<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  A CHILD SESSION, INVOKED BY probe_parent.scxml.

  It exists to answer ONE question about this tree: does `<invoke>` compile and
  run here, and does a child's `<donedata>` reach the parent as `_event.data`?
  The pinned SCE's own invoke tests are C++ AOT ones; nothing on the Rust path
  this workspace uses has ever driven one.

  Why the question is worth a document: the analysis a loop needs before it can
  improve its own context is a PROCESS with steps of its own, and a process with
  steps is a machine rather than a function. If a parent can own a child's
  lifecycle, that analysis is its own file which somebody can read and change
  without touching the loop.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       version="1.0"
       name="probe_child"
       datamodel="ecmascript"
       initial="running">

  <datamodel>
    <data id="answer" expr="'the child ran'"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT THE PARENT TOLD IT, and the second question this pair asks. The
      literal below is what a child gets when NOBODY told it anything — so a run
      that comes back carrying this string is a run where the parent's `<param>`
      did not cross, which is a different failure from the child not running.

      A composed design rests entirely on this: if decisions live in a parent
      document and topology in a child, the parent must be able to FILL the
      child's datamodel. If it cannot, the parent has nothing to say and the
      split has to be made some other way.
    -->
    <data id="told" expr="'nobody told this child anything'"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE SHAPE THE WHOLE SPLIT ACTUALLY NEEDS: A LIST OF OBJECTS. Every
      decision that would move from a template to a per-kind parent —
      `may_answer`, `screen_rules`, `judged_rules` — is exactly this shape, and a
      string crossing proves nothing about it.

      ⚠⚠ THE WORKSPACE HAS BEEN BITTEN HERE BEFORE: `sce-rust-lua` used to
      rewrite a JSON payload with `bytes[i] as char`, and valid JSON carrying an
      array or an escape was DEMOTED TO A STRING with no error raised. That was
      filed as PR-87 and fixed upstream — this data is what would notice it
      coming back.

      ⚠ The default is an EMPTY list, so a param that did not cross is told apart
      from one that crossed carrying nothing.
    -->
    <data id="rules" expr="[]"/>
  </datamodel>

  <state id="running">
    <onentry>
      <send event="finish"/>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="finish" target="over"/>
  </state>

  <final id="over">
    <donedata>
      <param name="carried" expr="answer"/>
      <!-- ⚠ ECHOED, not asserted here: a child cannot report its own datamodel
           to anybody but its parent, so the proof has to travel back out. -->
      <param name="heard" expr="told"/>
      <!-- ⚠ Echoed WHOLE rather than indexed. Indexing here would be a second
           question — the datamodel is evaluated as Lua whatever the attribute
           says — and this document is asking one. The Rust side walks it. -->
      <param name="heard_rules" expr="rules"/>
    </donedata>
  </final>
</scxml>
